  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 257 PM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThe new work week will start off on the hot and breezy side as \nthe area remains sandwiched between a potent (but progressive) \nshortwave trough over the northern Rockies and a strong upper \nridge over the Deep South. Deepening lee troughing, in response \nto the N Rockies trough, will lead to moderately breezy \nsoutherly winds and efficient mixing of an impressive late \nsummer low level thermal ridge. Latest NBM blend calls for high \ntemperatures in the 90s across the entire area. Far S/SE zones \nmay be even warmer given exceedance values of 100 degrees in the\n15-25 percent range. Another caveat to keep an eye on will be \nthe timing/placement of a cold front associated with \naforementioned trough. General consensus is that this front \nshould remain near or just N of the KS/NE state line through \npeak heating, but any minor trends one way or another could lead\nto forecast changes in these areas. Fire weather shouldn't \nbecome a concern as latest RH forecasts generally keep values at\nor above 25%. Also, wind speeds may actually decrease through \nthe afternoon (esp. central and northern zones) with closer \nproximity to the approaching cold front.\n\nMain question/potential weather impact for Monday evening and \novernight will be if deep convection can develop along the cold \nfront. If storms can indeed form, conceptual model would support\nhigh-based convection with potential to tap into hot/deeply \nmixed boundary layer (inverted-v profiles) and produce \nstrong/severe wind gusts. Moderately steep mid level lapse rates\n(~7.5 C/km), and hints of locally enhanced deep layer shear \nimmediately along the boundary (35-45kts?) could also support a \nlarge hail threat. It's not a forgone conclusion that everyone \nwill see storms/rain, though, as moisture/instability will be \nlacking (at least initially) and best upper forcing will slide \nacross the Dakotas...but this does look to be our next best \nchance for precipitation (60-80%).\n\nTuesday will be significantly cooler behind the front as latest\nensembles show 25th to 75th percentile temperatures mainly in \nthe 70s. Exception could be the far S/SE that still manage to \nwarm into the lower 80s. Regardless...solid 20-25 degrees cooler\nthan Monday. Cooler temperatures will also come with a \nrefreshing NE breeze. With drier air may come a brief taste of \n40s for esp. N/NW zones Wednesday AM. High pressure nosing in \nfrom the N. Plains should keep mild temperatures going through \nmidweek, but would expect some gradual air mass modification \nback to near or just above normal for the end of the week. \n\nExtended periods are littered with off an on POPs, though \nconfidence in any given period getting precipitation is low. The\narea will be located on the western side of a flattened upper \nridge that's centered over the Deep South. This will promote \nadvection of monsoonal upper moisture and at least a few weak \nperturbations in SW upper flow. These could support development \nof late afternoon convection along the Front Range that shifts E\nduring the evening, but again, confidence is low.",
